fferent folds, then average the best values. This prevents the thresholds from being overfit to a single hold-out split.\n- **Stability check / multiple random splits:** Try several different random train/val splits and check that the optimal T and N are stable across them. If the optimal value swings wildly, the sample size is too small to reliably tune on.\n- **Relation to the all-empty baseline:** The all-empty baseline achieves Score = 0.20 × 1.0 + 0.10 × 1.0 = 0.30 (since FgDice = 0 but FPS = 1 and EmptyAcc = 1). An aggressive threshold or large min-area filter can push predictions toward this baseline, inflating EmptyAccuracy and FalsePositiveScore while collapsing FgDice. If your tuned Score barely beats the all-empty baseline, you've likely overfit your post-processing to suppress all detections.\n\n### 3. Empty Mask Submission and Safety Checklist\n\n- **Empty mask encoding:** An entirely empty mask should have an **empty string** (or NaN/blank) in the `mask_rle` column of the submission CSV — no RLE tokens.\n- **Pre-submit checks:**\n  1. **Time budget:** Do a dry run and confirm total wall-clock time (all forward passes + I/O + RLE encoding) fits within the notebook limit (e.g., 30 minutes). Count: `n_models × n_views × n_images × 12 ms + overhead`.\n  2. **Memory budget:** Verify peak RAM usage. Use a running accumulator (one float32 prediction array) instead of storing all (model × view) predictions simultaneously. Check that the submission DataFrame itself doesn't duplicate large arrays.\n  3. **Submission format:** Verify the output CSV has exactly the expected number of rows (one per image-class pair), correct column names (`image_id`, `defect_type`, `mask_rle`), and no missing image-class pairs.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## Summary of All Answers\n\n| Q | Copy-Paste Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Q1 | `-0.8473, 2, 3, 0.8571, 0.4000` |\n| Q2 | `0.6730, 0.6908, 0.6120, Y, FalsePositiveScore and EmptyAccuracy` |\n| Q3 | `4, 5, 11, 